# paint/sound.planes — one helper per verb in planes-sound-protocol-v1.md,
# and nothing else (specification §9). The specification's verb table and this
# file are the same thing written twice; a test reads the table straight out of
# js/sound/protocol.mjs and asserts the correspondence verb for verb, no
# extras, no omissions. `protocol` itself is excluded — it is a stream
# directive, not a sounding verb, and a program states its version once,
# directly, as `show "sound protocol 1"`.
#
# TWO WORDS MOVED, AND BOTH MOVED IN THE SPECIFICATION TOO. `lasts`, not `for`:
# `for` is a reserved word in this language and a parameter cannot be named it.
# `silence`, not `clear`: `clear` is already a verb of the DRAWING protocol and
# paint/draw.planes already defines a helper of that name — and a program
# emitting both protocols, which is the entire point of the two prefixes,
# imports both libraries into one module graph, where two helpers of one name
# is a collision the loader refuses. Neither rename is local to this file: the
# specification and the library use the same words, so a collision in one
# renames both (planes-drawing-protocol-v2.md §6.5's rule, met again).
#
# `note` is not reserved and is safe to define: the parser reads the word
# `note` positionally, and only when it is immediately followed by `:` (a Note
# annotation block). `note of 3, 2, 1, 0, 0.4` is followed by `of`, so it is an
# ordinary call.

# `kind`, not `shape`: `shape` is a verb of the DRAWING protocol and
# paint/draw.planes defines a helper of that name, so a parameter called
# `shape` in a graph that imports both would resolve to that zero-argument
# function and quietly concatenate `nothing`.
to wave of kind:
  show "sound wave " + kind

to gain of level:
  show "sound gain " + text of level

to note of numerator, denominator, octave, at, lasts:
  show "sound note " + text of numerator + " " + text of denominator + " " + text of octave + " " + text of at + " " + text of lasts

to silence:
  show "sound silence"
